# Docs browser (`DOCS_ACCESS`)

| Mode | Public | Login required |
|------|--------|----------------|
| `protected` (default) | `/`, `/docs/md/*`, assets | `/docs/api`, `/docs/model/*`, `/model*`, `/docs/account/*` |
| `disabled` | same | model explorer → 404 |
| `public` | all | none |

Sign-in page (`/docs/login`) calls `POST /docs/session` with email/password and receives an HttpOnly session cookie plus a short-lived console key. If the account has MFA enabled, the response is `mfa_required` and the page prompts for a TOTP/backup code via `POST /docs/session/mfa`. The console key is kept in sessionStorage (with `console_key_id`) and revoked on logout. Each browser login gets its own console key (concurrent sessions are allowed); re-login in the same browser only revokes that browser’s prior key. The session endpoint uses login rate limiting (`login_rate_limit.ts`); handle HTTP 429 in `docs-login.js`.

Authenticated docs chrome shows an **Account** dropdown (Settings, API Keys, Sign Out). Settings (`/docs/account/settings`) manages password change, TOTP, and passkeys via the console API key against `/login/password`, `/login/totp/*`, and `/login/webauthn/*`.

If the ephemeral console key is missing from sessionStorage or revoked/expired on the server while a docs cookie remains, `api-console.js` must show the `#docs-reauth-modal` login challenge (not silently stay “signed in” with an empty key). `GET /docs/session` and docs middleware validate `console_key_id` via `resolveValidDocsSession` and clear the cookie when the key is gone.

## Restify

- Sync middleware: `(req, res, next)` + `res.redirect(302, url, next)`
- Async routes: `(req, res)` + `sendRedirect(res, url)` for redirects

## Tests

Run `npm run test` after changes. See `test/docs_auth.test.js`.
